#N canvas 20 20 1960 1700 12;
#X text 20 20 u_oled-preview -- shows what the patch draws to the OLED  \, off-device. Instantiated by u_mother-stub only. Eight boxes  \, one per 8-pixel ROW of the 128x64 screen  \, keyed by the y coordinate of each drawing command., f 58;
#X text 20 115 Not five boxes: screenLine1-5 is a DIFFERENT api  \, the five text lines mother.pd offers a patch  \, and u_mother-stub previews those separately. This is the graphics api  \, which is pixel-positioned. 64 pixels over an 8px font is 8 rows., f 58;
#X text 20 230 It shows WHAT is drawn and ROUGHLY where -- the row it starts on. Not a framebuffer: a 24px line covers three rows but only registers in the one it starts in., f 58;
#X obj 20 320 r oscOut;
#X obj 20 360 route sendtyped;
#X obj 800 410 print oscOut-not-sendtyped;
#X obj 20 410 route /oled/gPrintln /oled/gClear /oled/gFillArea /oled/gFlip /oled/gShowInfoBar /oled/gBox;
#X obj 1750 490 print oscOut-unhandled;
#X text 1750 540 gShowInfoBar and gBox are routed and DELIBERATELY not connected -- both go out on every frame and would drown the console. Swallowing them by name rather than by silence is what keeps the reject print below worth having: it now only fires for a command this previewer has never seen., f 28;
#X obj 20 490 list split 1;
#X obj 20 530 list split 2;
#X obj 20 570 t l l;
#X obj 20 610 list split 3;
#X obj 240 610 list split 1;
#X obj 240 650 / 8;
#X obj 240 690 i;
#X obj 20 730 list prepend;
#X obj 20 770 s \$0-rowmsg;
#X obj 560 490 t b b;
#X msg 700 530 0;
#X msg 560 530 8;
#X obj 560 570 until;
#X obj 560 610 f;
#X obj 560 650 t f f;
#X obj 720 690 + 1;
#X obj 560 730 list append .....................;
#X obj 560 770 s \$0-rowmsg;
#X obj 1000 490 list split 1;
#X obj 1000 530 list split 2;
#X obj 1000 570 list split 2;
#X obj 1000 610 unpack 0 0;
#X obj 1000 660 / 8;
#X obj 1000 700 i;
#N canvas 40 40 1100 900 bar 0;
#X text 20 20 A gFillArea width in pixels becomes a 21-character bar: n of = followed by 21-n of -. FIXED width on purpose. A bar that grows and shrinks the box is much harder to compare against the one above it  \, and comparing the two channels is the entire point of the home screen., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 inlet;
#X obj 20 170 * 21;
#X obj 20 210 / 128;
#X obj 20 250 i;
#X obj 20 290 clip 1 21;
#X obj 20 330 t f f b;
#X msg 500 380 list;
#X obj 500 420 s \$0-bclr;
#X obj 240 380 * -1;
#X obj 240 420 + 21;
#X obj 240 460 until;
#X obj 20 460 until;
#X obj 20 510 f 61;
#X obj 140 550 f 45;
#X obj 300 570 r \$0-bclr;
#X obj 20 600 list prepend;
#X obj 20 640 t l l;
#X obj 20 700 list tosymbol;
#X obj 20 740 outlet;
#X text 20 800 THE TRIGGER ORDER IS THE STRING ORDER. Clear the accumulator  \, prepend 21-n dashes  \, then prepend n equals in front of them. list prepend builds right to left  \, so doing the tail first is what puts the head in front of it., f 46;
#X text 420 800 clip 1 21 means a bar is never empty. g_oled already clips the pixel width to at least 1  \, so silence really does draw one pixel -- and a zero-length symbol here would leave the previous bar in place  \, which would be a lie., f 46;
#X text 820 800 61 is = and 45 is -. list tosymbol takes character codes  \, which is the only way vanilla Pd will build a symbol of a length it does not know in advance., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 2 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 6 1 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 6 0 12 0;
#X connect 11 0 14 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 13 0 16 0;
#X connect 14 0 16 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 1;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X connect 17 1 16 1;
#X connect 17 0 18 0;
#X connect 18 0 19 0;
#X restore 1220 660 pd bar;
#X obj 1000 760 pack 0 s;
#X obj 1000 800 s \$0-rowmsg;
#X obj 1500 490 t b;
#X obj 1500 530 f;
#X obj 1500 570 t f f;
#X obj 1660 610 + 1;
#X floatatom 1500 610 7 0 0 0 - - -;
#X text 20 830 gPrintln: drop the typetag  \, then screen and x -- what is left is y  \, height  \, colour and the words. y over 8 is the row., f 46;
#X text 420 830 gClear now BLANKS all eight rows \, and it does it by walking 0 to 7 through the same router the drawing commands use. Phase 1 deliberately did not blank -- with one layout the last text per row was the useful thing. With four layers a leftover row is a lie about which layer is showing., f 46;
#X text 820 830 gFillArea: the same header  \, then y and the width in pixels. The width becomes a bar \, the y becomes a row \, and pack puts them together -- unpack fires its right outlet first  \, so the bar is always ready before the row triggers the output., f 46;
#X text 1220 830 gFlip counts FRAMES now  \, which makes this a real frame-rate readout: it should climb by ten a second no matter how much traffic the disp bus is carrying. That is the whole claim of the arbiter  \, on screen., f 46;
#X obj 20 900 r \$0-rowmsg;
#X obj 20 940 route 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7;
#X obj 20 1000 list prepend set;
#X obj 20 1040 list trim;
#X msg 20 1080 .....................;
